[Pi](https://pi.dev) is a minimal coding agent for the terminal. You shape it to your workflow with packages that add extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. Two packages connect it to Appwrite: [pi-mcp-adapter](https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-mcp-adapter) adds MCP servers to your Pi sessions, and [pi-mcp-events](https://github.com/mikekelly/pi-mcp-events) lets Pi react to events those servers send.

# How does the integration work?

The [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers) connects Pi to your Appwrite project through the [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io). It is a hosted server at `https://mcp.appwrite.io/` that signs you in with OAuth, so you don't run a server on your machine or create an API key.

Once connected, Pi can look up your projects, read and change resources such as users, databases, and tables, and search the Appwrite documentation. Changes go through a separate write tool that requires confirmation, and each change carries a short reason that appears in your project's **Activity** tab.

The server also supports [MCP Events](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#events), an extension to the protocol. With pi-mcp-events installed, Pi can subscribe to a change in your project, such as a new user, finish its turn, and wake up when the event arrives over the connection it already has open, without a public URL.

{% info title="Self-hosted Appwrite" %}
The hosted MCP server authenticates against Appwrite Cloud, and events are only available on the hosted server. If you self-host Appwrite, use the [local MCP server](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/mcp) instead. It runs on your machine over stdio, connects to your instance with an API key, and does not support events.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To connect Pi to Appwrite, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Set up your Appwrite project

First, [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) if you haven't already, and create a project. You don't need an API key: Pi opens your browser so you can sign in to Appwrite.

## Step 2: Install Pi and the MCP adapter

If you don't have Pi yet, install it on macOS or Linux. On Windows, or to install with npm, see [pi.dev](https://pi.dev).

```bash
curl -fsSL https://pi.dev/install.sh | sh
```

Then install pi-mcp-adapter:

```bash
pi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter
```

## Step 3: Add the Appwrite MCP server

Add the Appwrite server to `~/.pi/agent/mcp-adapter.json`. Create the file if it doesn't exist, or merge this into it:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "appwrite": {
      "url": "https://mcp.appwrite.io/",
      "auth": "oauth"
    }
  }
}
```

Start Pi by running `pi`, then run `/mcp-auth appwrite`. Sign in to your Appwrite account in the browser window that opens and authorize access. The adapter keeps the sign-in in your operating system's credential store.

Pi also has built-in MCP support, which you set up with `pi mcp add`. This guide uses the adapter because the events extension needs it. The adapter turns off the built-in support and reads the servers you added with `pi mcp add`, so those keep working.

## Step 4: Test the integration

Run `/mcp` in Pi to check that **appwrite** is listed with its tools, then try prompts like:

- `Create a new user in my Appwrite project`
- `List all databases in my project`
- `Show me the tables in my database`

## Step 5: React to project events

Install pi-mcp-events, which adds an `mcp_events` tool on top of the adapter, then restart Pi to load it:

```bash
pi install npm:@realmikekelly/pi-mcp-events
```

Ask Pi to subscribe to new users. Replace `<PROJECT_ID>` with your project ID:

```text
Subscribe with mcp_events to users.user.created on the appwrite server for project_id <PROJECT_ID>, then finish your turn and wait. Whenever an event arrives, look up that user and tell me their name and email.
```

Pi opens the subscription and goes idle. When a user signs up, or you create one in the Appwrite Console, Pi wakes up without a new prompt, looks up the user, and replies with their name and email. Ask `Unsubscribe from users.user.created` when you're done.

Subscriptions last as long as the Pi session. After you restart Pi or run `/reload`, ask it to subscribe again. The [events reference](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#events) lists every event you can subscribe to, including failed function executions, completed deployments, new rows, and new files.

